When using the flatten operation, referencing a field (that contains an object) from an event results in a no-op. This is because when you reference a field from a vector event, you are just given the value, which in this case, is just an object without a key. Therefore, obviously, there is nothing to flatten.
I personally would expect the flatten operation to "just work" and flatten with the key into the main event.
Here's an example:
Event
{
    "timestamp": 1245542,
    "errors": {
        "code": 1234,
        "message": "invalid type",
        "class": "EventHelper"
    },
    "otherKey:" {
        "id": 1
    }
}

VRL
    flattened,err = flatten(.errors)

Expected:
{
    "timestamp": 1245542,
    "errors.code": 1234,
    "errors.message": "Invalid type",
    "errors.class": "EventHelper",
    "otherKey:" {
        "id": 1
    }
}

Actual:
No change to the event.

I have this VRL where the grok pattern for the log is based on the value of .fields.application. However, I am getting an error when using a variable for the pattern arguement.
Log sample:
{
  "message": "INFO - Application started successfully.",
  "fields": {
    "application": "app2"
  }
}
VRL:
dictionary = [
{ "parsing_type": "grok", "application": "app1", "pattern": "%{DATA:first_field},%{DATA:second_field},%{DATA:third_field}"},
{ "parsing_type": "grok", "application": "app2", "pattern": "%{LOGLEVEL:loglevel} - %{GREEDYDATA:message}"},
{ "parsing_type": "grok", "application": "app3", "pattern": "%{IPORHOST:ip}:%{NUMBER:port}"}
]

filter = filter(dictionary) -> |_index, value| {
    value.application == .fields.application
}

if length(filter) > 0 {
    config = filter[0]

    if config.parsing_type == "grok" {
        .log,err = parse_grok(.message, config.pattern)
    }

}

The error that I'm getting:
2023-09-18 18:06:55 15 │         .log,err = parse_grok(.message, config.pattern)
2023-09-18 18:06:55    │                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
2023-09-18 18:06:55    │                    │
2023-09-18 18:06:55    │                    unexpected expression for argument "pattern"
2023-09-18 18:06:55    │                    expected: literal
2023-09-18 18:06:55    │                    received: query

Tried different things with no luck. Any suggestions on how I can work around this?

